[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oggii/enshrouded-livemap/main/nexus/banner.jpg[/img][/center]

[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]
A live map of your server in the browser. Everyone with the link sees who is online and where, which way they are walking, the time of day, the weather, and every place the game marks on its map. It also remembers what your group finds: every ore vein someone mines and every chest someone opens shows up on the map for everybody.

[b]Server-side only.[/b] The mod runs inside the dedicated server and serves the map itself. Players install nothing; they open a link on PC, tablet or phone. When a player joins, the chat shows the link.

[size=4][b]New in 1.7.0[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]The game's own look:[/b] gold frames, separators and player arrows from the game files, a matching serif type, a compass rose and a scale in metres.[/*]
[*][b]Region names[/b] from the game's own map labels, large when you zoom out.[/*]
[*][b]Movable panels:[/b] drag the player list, clock or marker panel by its title; your browser remembers where.[/*]
[*][b]Phone layout:[/b] a compact clock and the marker panel as a sheet from the bottom.[/*]
[*][b]Smoother panning[/b] with all markers on, and [b]clay[/b] on the ore map.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Main features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]The game's own look:[/b] the map screen of the game in your browser: its gold frames, separators and player arrows, a matching serif type, a compass rose and a scale in metres. On phones the panels fold into a compact layout.[/*]
[*][b]Players live:[/b] names, positions and an arrow for the walking direction, updated four times a second. Click a name to follow that player; a link with &follow=<name> does it for a second screen.
[*][b]Death counter:[/b] every player's deaths next to their name, straight from the server: hover it to see how many were killed, fell, died in the shroud or drowned, and when the last one was. Kept across restarts.[/*]
[*][b]Time and weather:[/b] in-game clock with day and night, real time, and rain and snow drawn exactly where they fall, computed with the game's own weather rules (regions, fade in and out, snow line). Hover over the rain for its strength, time left and drift. One switch hides the weather.
[*][b]Deadly shroud:[/b] the shroud zones above your world's Flame level are drawn pink, as on the game's own map, with their own switch; hover a zone for its level. The Flame level comes straight from the server and also shows next to the clock.[/*]
[*][b]Region names:[/b] Springlands, Revelwood, Kindlewastes and the rest, the game's own region labels in large letters when you zoom out.[/*]
[*][b]All map markers from the game's own data:[/b] over 3,500 points in 28 categories with the game's icons, with the game's own names on hover ("Ancient Spire · Revelwood"), each category can be switched on and off: Ancient Spires and fast travel points (only the real destinations, as on the game's own map), Flame Shrines, Elixir Wells, towns, dungeons, Night Temples, caves, mines, Gem Forges, obelisks, hidden graves, Shroud nests, enemy camps, boss unlocks, gold, silver and wooden chests, sarcophagi, treasure fishing spots and more.
[*][b]Discovered places:[/b] show all places, only the discovered ones or only the ones not discovered yet, by anyone or by one player. Towns, dungeons, camps and the like use the game's own state, read from the server; every other marker counts as discovered within 300 m of where that player has been. Players keep their discoveries on the map after logging out. The tooltip says who discovered a place.[/*]
[*][b]Fog of war:[/b] darkens everything the chosen player has not explored yet, with a soft edge that clears live while they move. The game keeps its real fog on each player's PC, so the map builds its own from where the server saw them, with the game's own reveal radius of 300 m.[/*]
[*][b]Your Flame Altars live:[/b] every altar the players built shows up on the map with the game's altar icon and the size of its build zone, straight from the server. Build a new one and it appears within seconds; take one down and it disappears. The game's own starting altar is there too.
[*][b]Ore map that fills itself:[/b] when a player mines copper, iron, sulfur, flint, clay, crystals or any of 22 deposit kinds, the vein appears on the map with its own icon, who found it and how often it was mined. Shared by everyone, saved across restarts.
[*][b]Looted chests:[/b] a chest someone opened turns grey with a check mark (with name and time) and shows as full again after 30 minutes, when it refills (adjustable; chests inside a Flame Altar's build zone stay looted). One switch hides all looted chests.
[*][b]The game's own map:[/b] drawn from the map data inside the game (height, relief, contour lines, Shroud zones), sharp down to 1 metre per pixel when you zoom in. Only the part you look at is loaded.
[*][b]Fast:[/b] thousands of markers on one canvas, smooth panning and zooming, also on phones.
[*][b]Your layout:[/b] drag the player list, clock or marker panel by its title to where it suits you; your browser remembers it, a double-click puts it back.[/*]
[*][b]Optional HD map style[/b] (separate file): a colourful map picture by Nick, made sharp with the game's own relief. Switch between the game map and HD at the bottom left.
[*][b]Your own map picture:[/b] add any map image as a second style in [i]livemap/maps.json[/i]; a calibration helper lines it up.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Share the map[/b][/size]
The mod builds the link for you:
[list]
[*][b]Port:[/b] by default the same number as your game port, over TCP. Usually you only set your existing port forwarding to "TCP and UDP" (Docker: add [i]15637:15637/tcp[/i]). Rented servers with a Pterodactyl panel are often open for TCP already.
[*][b]Address:[/b] the mod finds your public IP by itself. Got a hostname or a tunnel with HTTPS? Set [i]PublicUrl=https://map.example.com[/i].
[*][b]Key:[/b] random on the first start, or any word you like: [i]Key=ourmap[/i]. Players can then type [i]your-address:15637/ourmap[/i].
[/list]
You find the full link in [i]livemap.log[/i] ("map link: ...") to post in your Discord, and the game chat shows it to everyone who joins.

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[b]Windows server[/b]
[list=1]
[*]Stop the server.
[*]Copy [i]iphlpapi.dll[/i] and the [i]livemap[/i] folder into the folder with [i]enshrouded_server.exe[/i].
[*]Start the server. The mod writes [i]livemap.ini[/i] and [i]livemap.log[/i].
[*]Open the port from [i]livemap.log[/i] for TCP (see "Share the map").
[/list]
[b]Linux / Docker (Wine)[/b]
[list=1]
[*]Stop the container.
[*]Copy [i]iphlpapi.dll[/i] and the [i]livemap[/i] folder next to [i]enshrouded_server.exe[/i].
[*]Add [i]iphlpapi=n,b[/i] to [i]WINEDLLOVERRIDES[/i] (with Shared XP: [i]WINEDLLOVERRIDES=powrprof=n,b;iphlpapi=n,b[/i]) and publish the TCP port.
[*]Start the container.
[/list]
[b]Optional HD map:[/b] unzip the optional file [i]HD map style[/i] into the same folder (it adds [i]livemap/tiles-hd[/i] and replaces [i]livemap/maps.json[/i]), reload the page. No restart needed.

[b]Check:[/b] [i]livemap.log[/i] shows "map link: http://..." and, after a player joins, the chat shows "Live map: ...". Open the link from outside your network (for example your phone without Wi-Fi).

[b]Uninstall:[/b] stop the server and delete iphlpapi.dll, the livemap folder, livemap.ini and livemap.log.

[size=4][b]Settings[/b][/size] ([i]livemap.ini[/i], restart the server after changes)
[list]
[*][b]Port[/b] (default auto): the game's port number over TCP, or a fixed number.
[*][b]PublicUrl[/b] (empty): your own address, e.g. https://map.example.com. Empty = public IP.
[*][b]DetectPublicAddress[/b] (1): 0 = never ask for the public IP.
[*][b]AnnounceInChat[/b] (1): the map link in the chat when a player joins.
[*][b]Key[/b] (random): 3 to 64 letters, digits, - or _; upper and lower case do not matter.
[*][b]Finds[/b] (1): ore deposits and looted chests.
[*][b]Bases[/b] (1): the players' Flame Altars.
[*][b]UpdateMs[/b] (250): how often the page gets new positions.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Good to know[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Everyone with the link sees all player positions and Flame Altars. Share it with your players only; change the key to lock old links out (or set Bases=0).
[*]Without a proxy or tunnel the map is plain HTTP.
[*]Links in the game chat cannot be clicked or copied: type the short link, or copy the full one from livemap.log.
[*]The ore map only shows deposits someone has mined on your server; ore veins are part of the terrain and not in the game's marker data.
[*]A chest counts as looted when someone opens it; a storage chest you build right next to a world chest can mark that chest as well.
[*]To find its public IP, the server asks api.ipify.org (or icanhazip.com) once an hour unless you set PublicUrl or DetectPublicAddress=0. Nothing else leaves your server.
[*][b]Safe on game updates:[/b] on an unknown server build the map stays off and the server runs as usual. Wait for an updated version.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Enshrouded 1.0 dedicated server, Steam builds [b]25988414[/b] and [b]26083076[/b] (October 2026).
[*]Windows server, or Linux/Docker under Wine (tested with mornedhels/enshrouded-server:1.7.2-wine).
[*]One TCP port that players can reach (the game port number by default).
[*]A current web browser for the players.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Works with[/b][/size]
[url=https://www.nexusmods.com/enshrouded/mods/178]Shared XP[/url] (1.1.3 or newer, also server-side only: non-combat XP for everyone online): both mods add their line to the chat when a player joins and run side by side.

Open source (MIT): [url=https://github.com/oggii/enshrouded-livemap]github.com/oggii/enshrouded-livemap[/url]. Some antivirus tools flag proxy DLLs by heuristics; you can read the code and build the DLL yourself.

